    {
      "id": "phrase-tomarse-algo-con-calma",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "tomarse algo con calma",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone handles something calmly without getting worked up?",
      "answerEn": "Use tomar with its matching me, te, se, nos, or os marker, name the situation, and then add con calma.",
      "noteEn": "Tomar normally means “to take.” In this expression it is pronominal: it comes with me, te, se, nos, or os even though no one literally takes themselves. Tomar changes to match the person and time, con calma describes the calm attitude, and the situation fills the algo, or “something,” slot.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Nos tomamos el cambio con calma.",
        "en": "We take the change calmly."
      },
      "tags": ["phrase", "attitude", "pronominal verbs", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-el-infinitive-as-noun",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "el + infinitivo usado como nombre",
      "questionEn": "How can Spanish use a verb to name an activity as a general idea?",
      "answerEn": "Put el before the verb's dictionary form, the unchanged form ending in -ar, -er, or -ir.",
      "noteEn": "The plus sign in the prompt means that el comes directly before the verb. That unchanged dictionary form is called the infinitive. With el in front, the verb names the activity like a masculine singular noun; this use often sounds more formal than saying directly what someone does.",
      "example": {
        "es": "El caminar ayuda a despejar la mente.",
        "en": "Walking helps clear the mind."
      },
      "tags": ["grammar", "infinitives", "nouns", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-quedarse-state",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "quedarse + palabra de estado",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone remains in, or ends up in, a particular state?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of quedarse followed by a word that names the resulting or continuing state.",
      "noteEn": "The plus sign in the prompt means that the state word follows quedarse. Quedarse is pronominal: it comes with me, te, se, nos, or os even though no one literally remains themselves. Change quedar and its small marker to match the person and time. When another verb has already changed for the person, the marker may stand before that verb: se puede quedar.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Después de la noticia, Pablo se quedó callado.",
        "en": "After the news, Pablo fell silent."
      },
      "tags": ["grammar", "states", "pronominal verbs", "A2"]
    }
  ],
